course: dictionary
lesson: 1119
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. A breadbasket can be a bowl on a table, but it can also be a whole area of land. «Ukraine is called the breadbasket of Europe» cannot be about a bowl, because a country cannot sit in a bowl. The words around it show it means a region that grows a lot of food.
explain: Check by swapping in your meaning. If you try «Ukraine is called the bowl for serving bread of Europe», it makes no sense, so the meaning must be a farming region. Think about what fits the whole sentence, not only the one hard word.
explain: Context can also show that a meaning is slang or old-fashioned. In a story, «a punch in the breadbasket» means a punch in the stomach. A reader who knows only the bowl meaning would be puzzled, but the words around it give the clue.
fact: Many words have more than one meaning. Context tells you which meaning is right.
fact: A breadbasket can be a basket or bowl for serving bread at the table.
fact: A breadbasket can also be a region that grows lots of grain, like wheat, and feeds many people.
fact: Chernozem is very rich black soil, found in Ukraine and southern Russia, and it helps farms grow wheat and sunflowers.
fact: In slang, and in old-fashioned stories, a breadbasket can mean your stomach.
exercise: «Ukraine is called the breadbasket of Europe.» What does breadbasket mean here? | A kind of soft white bread | A place where cakes are sold | *A region that grows lots of grain | A bowl for serving bread
exercise: «Ukraine has very rich black soil called chernozem.» Find and copy the word that names the black soil. | Europe | *chernozem | Ukraine | sunflowers
exercise: In a story, «He got a punch in the breadbasket.» Where did the punch land? | On his hat | In a bread shop | *In his stomach | On his foot
test: «He got a punch in the breadbasket.» Why is the meaning not a bowl? | Because breadbasket is a longer word in a story | *Because the words around it show a body, not a table | Because punches are always thrown at bowls | Because the sentence is about a meal
test: Put the meaning «bowl for bread» into «Ukraine is called the breadbasket of Europe.» Does it make sense? | *No, so the meaning must be a farming region | Yes, because Europe is a bowl | Yes, a country can sit in a bowl | Not sure, so I will guess
exam: Which meaning fits «the breadbasket of the country» best, and why? | A bread shop, because bread is sold there | A bowl for bread, because the sentence is about a meal | Your stomach, because the country is hungry | *A region that grows lots of grain, because the sentence is about farming land
